<h2>A 1990 marriage, a 2016 petition, a 2026 appeal</h2>
<p>The parties married in 1990 and have an adult son. In 2016 the husband petitioned for divorce before the Family Court, Mandi, alleging roughly two decades of separate living, neglect, and the filing of false proceedings under Section 498-A IPC and the Domestic Violence Act. The wife&rsquo;s answer was concrete: he drank, quarrelled and beat her; compromises were brokered before the police and the Gram Panchayat; and he ultimately turned her out into an old cowshed or room without basic facilities adjacent to the house. The Family Court dismissed the petition with costs in 2019, answering both the cruelty and desertion issues against the husband; the Division Bench has now affirmed, finding he <em>&ldquo;miserably failed&rdquo;</em> to prove either ground.</p>

<h2>Why each ground failed</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Ground</th><th>The husband&rsquo;s case</th><th>The Court&rsquo;s answer</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Cruelty by complaints</td><td>The 498-A case and DV proceedings were false and amounted to mental cruelty</td><td>Complaints <em>&ldquo;do not ipso facto constitute mental cruelty&rdquo;</em>; only proceedings <em>&ldquo;proved to be false, frivolous, or malicious&rdquo;</em> qualify — and no court ever found these false</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cruelty generally</td><td>Years of neglect and misbehaviour</td><td>Allegations were general, without dates or particulars, and his sole testimony was uncorroborated (Suman Singh v. Sanjay Singh applied)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Desertion</td><td>Separate living for over two decades</td><td>Constructive desertion: his conduct forced her out, so animus deserendi was missing; Section 23(1)(a) bars advantage from one&rsquo;s own wrong</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Credibility</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>He feigned ignorance of the 498-A case in deposition despite admitting he attended its proceedings — the false denial undermined his whole account</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The finding that reframes the case</h2>
<p>The most striking passage concerns the washroom. The 2014 compromise deed recorded the husband&rsquo;s undertaking to let the wife use the toilet and washroom of the house — an undertaking whose necessity spoke for itself. The Bench concluded: <em>&ldquo;Such conduct amounts to clear cruelty committed by the petitioner-husband against the respondent-wife.&rdquo;</em> The irony is complete: the only cruelty established in a husband&rsquo;s cruelty petition was his own. On desertion, the Court held the wife <em>&ldquo;cannot be heard to contend that she has deserted him&rdquo;</em> — the respondent obviously being entitled to that defence where the petitioner authored the separation.</p>

<h2>Lessons for matrimonial litigators</h2>
<p>Three points deserve a place in every cruelty-desertion brief. Particularise: stale and general allegations fail under Suman Singh, and a petitioner&rsquo;s uncorroborated word rarely carries a two-decade narrative. Audit the paper trail before filing: compromise deeds, panchayat records and police settlements are admissions that will be put to the petitioner in cross-examination, and they bind. And treat pending or withdrawn criminal proceedings realistically: unless a judicial finding of falsity exists or can be obtained, building a mental-cruelty case on the mere fact of a 498-A or DV complaint invites exactly the analysis this judgment applies.</p>
<div class="note">Matrimonial findings are specific to the evidence led; the decision does not hold that complaints can never constitute cruelty — proof of falsity or malice remains the dividing line. Case details are as recorded in the judgment dated 29 September 2026.</div>
